    {
      "body": "@YifanLi3 A data point for change 2, from a case where the new text's stated cause is not the cause.\n\nOne NVIDIA L4 (23034 MiB), vLLM 0.28.0, `--gpu-memory-utilization 0.95 --max-num-seqs 1`, two 4-bit models on the same card the same day:\n\n```\ngemma-4-31B  w4a16   Model loading took 18.7 GiB    Available KV cache memory: -0.8 GiB\n        + --enforce-eager                           Available KV cache memory:  1.71 GiB  (2,020 tokens)\n\nQwen3.8-27B  int4    Model loading took 19.24 GiB   Available KV cache memory: -0.39 GiB\n        + --enforce-eager                           Available KV cache memory: -0.34 GiB\n```\n\nFor the first, **the model is not too large** — it serves, at 2,020 tokens of context. What put the budget below zero was the CUDA graphs, worth **2.51 GiB** here. None of the three suggestions reaches that: it is already a quantized checkpoint, so (1) is met, and (3) is already at 0.95. The lever that worked is `--enforce-eager`, and it is not on the list.\n\nFor the second the new text is right, and that is the difficulty — the same flag on the same card buys **0.05 GiB**, and 19.24 GiB of weights really do leave too little. Two cases one log line apart, wanting opposite advice.\n\nSo: a fourth item naming `--enforce-eager` (or a smaller capture set) would cover the first case. And the message may be able to tell them apart rather than guessing, since the profiler already estimates the graph footprint — #51590 is about making that estimate right — so it could say whether giving graphs up would cover the deficit.\n\nFour serve logs, two per model: https://github.com/cadamcat/dual-radeon-vllm/tree/main/benchmarks/cuda-l4/campaign-2026-08-30c/logs",
      "created_at": "2026-08-30T20:58:37Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/50985#issuecomment-5471218129",
      "id": 5471218129,
      "updated_at": "2026-08-30T20:58:37Z",
